arXiv:2607.11499v3 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: In 2017, Keith defined and studied a multi-restricted partition function $RD^{l,t}(n)$, the number of integer partitions of n into parts that are simultaneously $l$-regular and $t$-distinct if none of the parts is divisible by $l$ and parts appear fewer than $t$ times. Thereafter, in a series of papers Buragohain et al. and Nadji et al. examined combinatorial and arithmetic properties of $RD^{l,t}(n)$. In this article, we focus on simultaneously $l$-regular and $t$-distinct partitions in 3-colors, $RD^{l,t}_3(n)$ and establish infinite families of congruences modulo powers of 3 for specific values of $(l,t)$. For instance, for positive integers $n$ and $k$, we have
  \begin{align*}
  \sum_{n=0}^{\infty}RD_3^{3,3}\left(3^kn+\frac{3^k+1}{2}\right)q^n\equiv0 \pmod{3^{k+1}}.
  \end{align*}
  Our approach involves Hirschhorn's H operator technique.

arXiv:2610.12831v3 Announce Type: replace-cross 
Abstract: Given an integer $k \geq 2$, there are integers $M_k$ and $P_k$ so that for all $A \subseteq \mathbb F^n_p$ does not contain a restricted polynomial progression of the form
  \begin{align*}
  x, x+\begin{pmatrix}
  y_1 \\ y_2 \\ \vdots \\ y_n
  \end{pmatrix}, x+\begin{pmatrix}
  y_1^2 \\ y_2^2 \\ \vdots \\ y_n^2
  \end{pmatrix}, \ldots, x+\begin{pmatrix}
  y_1^{k-1} \\ y_2^{k-1} \\ \vdots \\ y_n^{k-1}
  \end{pmatrix},
  \end{align*}
  where $x \in \mathbb F^n_p$, with $p>P_k$, and $y \in \{0,2, 3, \ldots, M_k\}^n \backslash \{\vec{0} \}$, for some fixed $M_k$, then $A$ must have density bounded above by $\frac{C}{(\log^{(k^{O(k)})} n)^c}$, where $C, c>0$ only depend on $k$ and $p$. For $k\geq 4$, the best previously known bound came from the density Hales--Jewett theorem and was of inverse Ackermann type. The proof uses a recent inverse theorem for noise stability of Bhangale, Khot, Liu and Minzer, which arose in the study of constraint satisfaction problems. The main new ingredient is a proof that the underlying distribution counting restricted polynomial progressions does not admit a nontrivial abelian embedding, by way of an involved algebraic argument. Reasonable bounds are provided for $P_k$ and $M_k$.
